r fundraising signals available, the forecast rests on West Virginia's strong Republican fundamentals; the state's 21-point GOP lean points to a lopsided Republican margin, and recent coverage offers no sign of a competitive dynamic.", "watch": "The first credible in-state polls or a dramatic shift in the national environment could move the number, though closing a 21-point gap would require an extraordinary swing.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "West Virginia sits deep in Republican territory, with a partisan lean of roughly 21 points toward the GOP, and no incumbent president is on the ballot this cycle. No public polls of the state or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying partisanship, which strongly favors the Republican nominee.\n\nHeadlines offer little to suggest a competitive race: coverage centers on 2028 primary calendar questions, Joe Manchin's new independent leadership council, and Kentucky Governor Andy Beshear's rising popularity as a potential Democratic contender. None of that changes the structural picture. The Republican side starts with a commanding advantage, and a Democratic win would require a national shift far larger than anything currently on the horizon.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0132, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0348, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0377, "margin": -21.0}]}